[gd_resource type="Resource" script_class="Conversation" format=3]

[ext_resource type="Script" path="res://game/dialogue/conversation.gd" id="1_convo"]

[resource]
script = ExtResource("1_convo")
id = &"n3_anselm"
text = "# DRAFT
# Anselm at dusk, in the infirmary (night 3). The old porter, blind, hunched in his chair, his stick across his knees.
# Guilty and terrified: he let victims in for years. He won't say so, and nothing here says so.
== start
(The old man in the chair turns his face to the door before you reach it.)
Anselm: That's the porter's step. You walk like a man who's been told not to run.
Anselm: Still with us, then. Good. Sit, if you like.
-> topics

== again
(His milky eyes settle on a place just beside your face.)
Anselm: Back again? Nobody else comes twice. Sit.
-> topics

== topics
* [if not n3_anselm_bell] What did you mean, don't let them in after the bell? -> bell
* [if not n3_anselm_gate] Tell me about keeping the gate, brother. -> gate
* [if not n3_anselm_eyes] How did your eyes go, Brother Anselm? -> eyes

== bell [set n3_anselm_bell]
(His hand finds your sleeve and grips it. He listens to the room before he speaks.)
Anselm: I meant what I said. No more than that.
Anselm: Bar it at the bell and keep it barred. Whoever knocks. Whatever they tell you.
Anselm: Don't ask me why, boy. I'm old, and I can't see who's listening.
-> topics

== gate [set n3_anselm_gate]
Anselm: Thirty years I kept it. I knew every knock in the ward by the sound.
Anselm: The carter's, the bailiff's, Walter's. Pilgrims knock soft, as if the gate might bite.
Anselm: I let in whoever came. That was the rule I was given, and I kept it.
(He's quiet a while.)
Anselm: Write them all down, boy. Every one. Somebody should.
-> topics

== eyes [set n3_anselm_eyes]
Anselm: They went like milk in a pail. Grey at the edges first, then the middle. Two winters.
Anselm: I hear better than I did. That's the bargain God makes with you.
Anselm: The night before last, I heard the tower creak before ever you rang. There was no wind. Only the rain.
-> topics
"
